<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  CycloneDDS configuration for the x86 HOST side.

  Mounted read-only at /cfg/cyclonedds.xml and selected with
  CYCLONEDDS_URI=file:///cfg/cyclonedds.xml.

  THIS IS THE TEMPLATE. The compose file mounts `host.rendered.xml`, which
  `scripts/module.sh sync` generates from this file by adding the module's
  address and this host's interface. The generated file is not versioned
  because addresses do not go into git (CLAUDE.md conventions).

  CONSEQUENCE THAT COST A SESSION, 25/08/2026: editing this file has NO effect
  on the containers until `module.sh sync` is run. That bench's rendered file
  had been generated earlier, was byte-identical to this one, and stayed put
  while this one changed. After any edit here:

      scripts/module.sh sync     # regenerates host.rendered.xml
      docker compose -f compose.host.yml up -d  (with force-recreate)

  A structural test in tests/ fails a rendered file older than this one, so
  that this divergence cannot stay silent.

  scripts/env.sh points at the rendered file when it exists, and at this file
  when it does not, so that a native host tool and a container speak the SAME
  discovery policy. Two policies on the same domain work as long as the
  interface picked by accident happens to match, and stop without warning when
  it stops matching.

  NOTE ON INTERFACE SELECTION
  ===========================
  The ML3.5 guide (§5) shows <NetworkInterface name="eth0"/>. That is a
  PLACEHOLDER and it is wrong on this workstation, which has no eth0 at all:
  `ip -br link` here reports lo, enp0s31f6 (DOWN, no carrier), wlp0s20f3 (UP,
  Wi-Fi), tailscale0, docker0 and bridges.

  Hardcoding a name that does not exist makes CycloneDDS fail to find the
  interface and fall back in a way that looks like a DDS problem rather than a
  config typo. So this file uses autodetermine instead of a name.

  autodetermine picks the highest-priority interface CycloneDDS can find. That is
  usually right on a single-NIC machine and NOT reliably right here, where
  docker0 and tailscale0 are also up and can be chosen ahead of the intended
  link. If host<->module discovery misbehaves, replace the autodetermine line
  with the real interface name, verified first:

      ip -br link                      # on this host
      # then, in this file:
      # <NetworkInterface name="wlp0s20f3" priority="default"/>

  Multicast is off and peers are explicit because multicast commonly dies on
  Wi-Fi and on managed switches. This host is on Wi-Fi (wlp0s20f3), so that is
  the expected case, not a corner case.
-->
<CycloneDDS xmlns="https://cdds.io/config">
  <Domain id="any">
    <General>
      <Interfaces>
        <!--
          LOOPBACK FIRST, AND IT IS THE 25/08/2026 FIX.

          The whole learn-mode stack runs with network_mode: host on the SAME
          machine, so the correct path between containers is `lo`. Before this
          line, traffic depended on whatever the autodetermine below picked, and
          the header of this file already predicted the problem: with docker0,
          two bridges and tailscale0 up, the choice can land on an interface
          that does not route to the other participants.

          WHAT IT CAUSED, MEASURED
          ASYMMETRIC delivery between containers. `nav` received /demo/odom and
          /demo/scan_cloud from `sim` (sim's locators were reachable) and `sim`
          did NOT receive /demo/cmd_vel from `nav`. The result does not look
          like a network problem: Nav2 plans, commands yaw in 95.6% of the
          samples, and the robot trots IN PLACE, i.e. `sticks=(lx=0.0000
          ly=0.0000 rx=0.0000)` in the gait log for 180 s, with the yaw frozen.
          Nav2 then reports "Failed to make progress" and enters recovery, which
          does not arrive either. Zero lines mentioning DDS or the interface.

          The log does fill up with `ddsi_udp_conn_write ... failed with
          retcode -1` for an address of the host itself, but that is millions of
          low-level lines in the middle of Gazebo output, not an error anyone
          reads.

          `lo` exists on every machine, so this is NOT the hardcoded name the
          header above condemns; it is the only interface name that is safe to
          hardcode. Multicast is enabled only here: on loopback it does not
          cross any switch, so the "dies on Wi-Fi" objection does not apply.

          The real interface stays in the list, below, so that hil mode can
          reach the module. DO NOT REMOVE EITHER OF THE TWO: without `lo` the
          asymmetry above returns; without the real one the host does not talk
          to the Aquila.

          THE PRIORITIES IN THIS COPY ARE FOR LEARN. `module.sh sync` generates
          the HIL variant and inverts the priorities: Ethernet=10, lo=default.
          It was measured on 28/08/2026 that keeping lo=10 in HIL makes Cyclone
          try external unicast over the loopback (`ddsi_udp_conn_write ...
          retcode -3`): /clock shows up in the remote graph, but no sample
          crosses.
        -->
        <NetworkInterface name="lo" priority="10" multicast="true"/>
        <NetworkInterface autodetermine="true" priority="default"/>
      </Interfaces>
      <!--
        Off deliberately. With multicast enabled and peers listed, discovery can
        succeed over multicast on the bench and then fail on the customer's
        network, which hides the misconfiguration until the demo is in front of
        someone.
      -->
      <AllowMulticast>false</AllowMulticast>
    </General>
    <Discovery>
      <ParticipantIndex>auto</ParticipantIndex>
      <Peers>
        <!--
          127.0.0.1 IS LOAD-BEARING. DO NOT REMOVE IT.

          With AllowMulticast=false, CycloneDDS discovers ONLY what is listed
          here — and that includes participants in the same container and in
          sibling containers on the same machine. Without a localhost peer there
          is no same-machine discovery path at all.

          Measured, learn mode, all four containers up: without this line
          `ros2 node list` returned nothing, `ros2 topic list` showed only
          /rosout and /parameter_events, and the Gazebo spawner sat forever on
          "Waiting messages on topic [robot_description]" while
          robot_state_publisher was demonstrably running and had logged "Robot
          initialized" in the same container. Nothing named DDS as the cause.

          Note that this is NOT the loopback interface. CycloneDDS selects the
          highest-quality interface (here wlp0s20f3) and transmits on it; the
          localhost peer only adds a discovery address. Forcing
          <NetworkInterface name="lo"/> instead was tested and is WRONG — it
          isolates the client from nodes that already selected the real
          interface.

          THE MODULE IP IS NOT LISTED HERE.
          The ${MODULE_IP} placeholder that used to sit in this file never
          expanded: Docker does not perform variable substitution inside a
          bind-mounted file, so CycloneDDS received the literal string
          "${MODULE_IP}" as an address. The ML3.5 guide §5 shows it that way and
          it cannot work as written.

          For hil mode (F5), add the module's real address as another <Peer>,
          either by editing this file on the host or by generating it from .env
          at compose time. That is F5 work; F1 is host-only and needs only
          localhost.
        -->
        <Peer address="127.0.0.1"/>
      </Peers>
    </Discovery>
  </Domain>
</CycloneDDS>
